## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference.

Every render creates **new references** for inline objects and functions, so even if the data is identical, the child sees a "different" prop and re-renders.

```jsx
// Bad: new object reference on every parent render
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// Bad: new function reference on every parent render
<Child onClick={() => handleClick(id)} />
```

The child component has no way to know the values are semantically the same — the reference changed, so it re-renders.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Hook/API | What it memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | The component itself | Child is expensive to render and receives stable props |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Computation is expensive or result is passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function is passed as prop to a memoized child |

**Key rule:** `useCallback`/`useMemo` are only useful when the result goes to a `React.memo` component or another hook dependency array. Memoizing in isolation does nothing.

```jsx
// useCallback is pointless here — Child is not memoized
const onClick = useCallback(() => doThing(), []);
return <Child onClick={onClick} />;  // Child still re-renders

// useCallback is useful here — Child is memoized
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => <button onClick={onClick} />);
const onClick = useCallback(() => doThing(), []);
return <Child onClick={onClick} />;  // Child skips re-render ✓
```

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value is compared by reference too. If the provider creates a new object inline, **every consumer re-renders on every parent render** — even if the data didn't change.

```jsx
// Bad: new object every render → all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix:** memoize the context value, or split stable and volatile state into separate contexts.

```jsx
// Better: value only changes when user or setUser changes
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
return <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>;

// Best for large trees: split into two contexts
// UserContext (changes) and UserDispatchContext (stable — setUser never changes)
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Parent` re-renders, even when `items` hasn't changed.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filter = { active: true };               // new ref every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => setSelected(id);  // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList filter={filter} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ filter, onSelect }) {
  console.log('rendered'); // fires on every parent render
  // ...expensive work
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filter` or `onSelect` actually change.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filter = useMemo(() => ({ active: true }), []);       // stable ref
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => setSelected(id), []); // stable ref

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList filter={filter} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ filter, onSelect }) {
  console.log('rendered'); // only fires when filter or onSelect change
  // ...expensive work
});
```

---

**Bottom line:** measure before optimizing — React DevTools Profiler shows which components re-render and why. Memoization adds complexity; only reach for it when a component is genuinely expensive or causes visible performance problems.
